For every smooth positive weight $u$ on $\Sigma$, there is a map $T_{\tau,a}$ in <a href="#eq:index-conformal-map" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:index-conformal-map">[eq:index-conformal-map]</a> such that $$\label{eq:index-balance}
 \int_\Sigma u\,T_{\tau,a}(x)\,dV=0.$$ For every $a\in S^{m+1}$ and $\tau\ge0$, $$\label{eq:index-conformal-area}
 \int_\Sigma\beta_{\tau,a}^{\,m}\,dV\le V_\Sigma,$$ with strict inequality if $\tau>0$. Consequently, $$\label{eq:index-conformal-energy}
 \int_\Sigma\beta_{\tau,a}^{\,2}\,dV\le V_\Sigma,$$ again with strict inequality if $\tau>0$.

Let $p=m+3$ and $$Q=\overline B^{m+2}\times[-1,1],\qquad u=(v,b).$$ Identify opposite points of $\partial Q$, and denote the quotient map by $q:Q\longrightarrow\mathbb {RP}^{p}$. There is a flat-continuous map $$\Phi:\mathbb {RP}^{p}\longrightarrow
 \mathcal Z_m^0(S^{m+1};\mathbb Z_2)$$ with the following properties.*


1.  *The map $\Phi$ is detecting in the sense of Definition <a href="#def:detecting" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:detecting">12</a>, has no concentration of mass, and satisfies $$\Phi(q(0,0))=\partial[\Omega],\qquad
     \mathbf M(\Phi(q(0,0)))=|\Sigma|,$$ where $\Omega$ is the side of $\Sigma$ opposite to $N$. For $r=\sqrt{1-b^2}$ its masses obey $$\label{eq:family-mass-bounds}
     \mathbf M(\Phi(q(v,b)))\le
     \begin{cases}
      r^m|\Sigma|,& |v|<1,\\
      r^m\sigma_m,& |v|=1.
     \end{cases}$$ In particular $|\Sigma|\ge\gamma_m>\sigma_m$, where $\gamma_m$ is the constant in Lemma <a href="#lem:detection-gap" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:detection-gap">14</a>.*


2.  *The central parameter is the unique maximum, in the uniform sense that for every neighborhood $U$ of $(0,0)$ in $Q$, $$\label{eq:family-away-gap}
     \sup_{u\in Q\setminus U}\mathbf M(\Phi(q(u)))<|\Sigma|.$$*


3.  *Near $(0,0)$ these cycles are precisely the multiplicity-one boundaries of a smooth family of domains, with embedded boundaries parametrized by $\Sigma$. The normal derivative at the center, in the parameter direction $(d,t)\in\mathbb R^{m+2}\times\mathbb R$, is $$\label{eq:family-normal-variations}
     w_{d,t}(x)=t+\langle d,x\rangle.$$ These variations form a $p$-dimensional subspace on which the area second variation is negative definite.*


4.  *If $\mathop{\mathrm{ind}}(\Sigma)>p$, there is a flat homotopy of $\Phi$, supported in the image of a neighborhood of $(0,0)$, to a detecting map $\widetilde\Phi$ with no concentration of mass and $$\label{eq:family-strict-deformation}
     \sup_{\xi\in\mathbb {RP}^{p}}\mathbf M(\widetilde\Phi(\xi))<|\Sigma|.$$

There is $K<1$ such that $$\label{eq:family-spacelike-bound}
 |\varphi(z)|\le K|z|\qquad(z\in\mathbb R^{m+2}).$$ For $z\ne0$, put $h=\varphi(z)$. Every closest point of $\mathcal C$ to $z$ has the form $r_zx$, with $x\in\Sigma$ and $$\label{eq:family-contact}
 r_z=\sqrt{|z|^2-h^2}>0,\qquad
 z=r_zx+hN(x),\qquad r_z I-hA(x)\ge0.$$ The final inequality is an inequality of self-adjoint endomorphisms of $T_x\Sigma$.

Every $E_{v,b}$, for $(v,b)\in Q$, has finite perimeter. For $|v|<1$, $|b|<1$ and every open set $U\subset S^{m+1}$, $$\label{eq:family-restricted-perimeter}
 \begin{split}
 \operatorname{Per}(E_{v,b};U)
 &\le\mathcal H^m(\Lambda_{v,b}\cap U)\\
 &\le\int_{T_{v,b}\cap Y_{v,b}^{-1}(U)}
       \det(rI-h_{v,b}A)\,d\mu,
 \end{split}$$ and on $T_{v,b}$, $$\label{eq:family-restricted-density}
 0\le\det(rI-h_{v,b}A)\le r^m\le1.$$ Thus these estimates hold at every level, without an exceptional set of values of $b$.

The family $\widehat\Phi$ satisfies $$\label{eq:family-no-concentration}
 \lim_{\rho\downarrow0}\;
 \sup_{(v,b)\in Q}\sup_{y\in S^{m+1}}
 \|\widehat\Phi(v,b)\|(B_\rho(y))=0.$$

Let $V$ be a stationary integral $k$-varifold in an open set $U\subset\mathbb R^n$. At every $z\in\mathop{\mathrm{spt}}\|V\|$, $\Theta(V,z)\geq1$. If $V=\mathcal C$ is a cone in all of $\mathbb R^n$, then $$\label{eq:cones-density-comparison}
 1\leq\Theta(\mathcal C,z)\leq\Theta(\mathcal C,0)
 \qquad (z\in\mathop{\mathrm{spt}}\|\mathcal C\|).$$ Every tangent cone at such a point is nonzero and has density $\Theta(\mathcal C,z)$ at its vertex.
